So we'll be in London, New York, San Francisco, Berlin later this year.

11
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:05,440
So if you haven't heard of the Subclub Podcast, I talk to industry experts like Ramit every two weeks on the podcast.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,600
And so looking forward to this chat today.

13
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:13,440
I think there's a lot we can learn from one of the leaders of some of the biggest apps in the app stores.

14
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:15,200
So yeah, my guest is Ramit.

15
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,800
He's a lead PM on monetization at Microsoft covering Office 365 and Copilot.

16
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,360
Yeah, and thanks for joining me on the podcast today.

17
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:24,080
Thank you.

18
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:24,720
Thank you so much.

19
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:32,880
So yeah, I essentially am the growth leader for Microsoft 365 apps for macOS and I support growth for the mobile apps.

20
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:41,760
So essentially the core apps Word, Excel, PowerPoint and also supporting the Copilot app and the Microsoft 365 app as well.

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,480
So yeah, all of them big apps.

22
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:52,320
So yeah, excited to talk about them and all views are my own and do not represent Microsoft's official stance.

23
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:53,920
I'm supposed to say that.

24
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,280
So okay.

25
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:56,480
Of course, of course.

26
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:15,680
One of the things when you and I were first starting talking about having this conversation today that really struck me about our early conversations is that it really sounds like you and your team and even the kind of app team inside Microsoft is like a startup inside a startup inside the biggest company in the world.

27
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:22,480
I always thought of Microsoft as enterprise and people go to the web to download Word and it's a native app on Mac.

28
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,240
I kind of always thought the apps were almost an afterthought.

29
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:33,600
But the more we talked, it's like, I mean, Microsoft is very serious about building fantastic apps and monetizing them on the app stores.

30
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:33,920
Yes.

31
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,400
So tell me a little bit about that kind of approach.

32
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:41,840
and how do you think about growing an app business inside the biggest company in the world?

33
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:50,000
In the very, say, like 2013, 2014 era, these apps started as companion apps to the desktop apps.

34
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:59,120
So there's always this concept of desktop class productivity, which is that you have a screen, which is, say, an iPad or a desktop, and that's where work gets done.

35
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:02,720
And then there's mobile productivity, where you have light edits.

36
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:14,560
And then over time, we start to see that mobile being the primary device of a lot of customers, and this is more true in Asia and other regions than US, but mobile being the primary device and not a shared device.

37
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:22,080
So people have, say, in one family, there would be like two, like everyone will have a phone and maybe they will have one laptop.

38
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:29,680
So we started to see people doing very long form documents, like sometimes up to fifty pages of documents from their mobile phones.

39
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,240
So that's when we started to get very serious about this.

40
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:53,360
And then the other trend that we saw was that because App Store has a very, very frictionless experience for buying subscription, we started to see that Microsoft 365 subscription, especially in consumer, like our personal and family subscriptions, and now Copilot Pro keeps seeing this trend that people just want to make the purchase from their phone.

41
00:03:53,920 --> 00:04:03,200
It's kind of like how a lot of people buy from the Amazon app rather than from the website because it's just a more handy device, this when your credit card is attached.

42
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:11,040
And we see almost a five X higher trial to paid conversion rate on mobile than on other direct channels sometimes.

43
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:17,840
Because of very obvious reasons like this, credit card is already attached and there is a frictionless experience.

44
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:23,440
People trust providers like Apple, Google, Amazon to essentially manage their subscription.

45
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:37,520
So while we have a very large direct business, we start to see that App Store has this real potential in terms of very high trial to paid credit card conversion already attached, people having this as their primary device.

46
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:40,000
That's where they're managing a lot of subscriptions from.

47
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:42,000
So those were some of the things.

48
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:44,160
And then we started to get very serious.

49
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:53,680
And App Store just gave us so much organic traffic that sure we pay some percentage to the App Store, but wherever you acquire, you have to pay some percentage.

50
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,520
So basically, the organic traffic just blew our mind.

51
00:04:57,760 --> 00:04:59,840
And so we just invested and here we are.

52
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:03,120
It's so funny because Apple's so protective of their percentage.

53
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:12,080
And I think people discount just how much it being so convenient is actually increasing conversion rates and leading to new subscribers who just wouldn't subscribe on the web.

54
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:18,800
So you hadn't told me that before sitting here on the stage that you'd see a five X increase in conversion for trial.

55
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:19,920
That's incredible.

56
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:20,480
Yeah, yeah.

57
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:24,000
In some platforms, I mean, obviously we sell across multiple platforms.

58
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:28,160
I think only on consumer, we have like ten different channels that we are selling on.

59
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:35,280
So we have other channels that also perform very well, but App Store is one of the highest performing in terms of trial to paid conversion specifically.

60
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:36,160
Yeah, that's amazing.

61
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:48,400
And how do you view competing on the App Store though as far as there are so many productivity apps, you know, so many notes apps, so many places that you can create documents and everything like that.

62
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:57,200
How does a massive brand like Microsoft approach competing in this marketplace that is very different from the web?

63
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:01,200
Yeah, I mean, it has been a bit of a learning for sure.

64
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:22,160
But one of the things I will say, so competing on App Store, you just have to operate like a startup because if you see any other distribution channel like a web or say even retail partnerships, those kind of things, we have some advantage there that, okay, we have an exclusive deal with a retailer or we have a website that has say millions of users that are coming on on App Store.

65
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:23,120
It's very democratic.

66
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:26,160
App Store, I like to think about it like YouTube is, right?

67
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:31,680
Like Paramount Pictures and a YouTube influencer, they're pretty much competinging for the same eyeball.

68
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,920
So we don't get a lot of advantage.

69
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:37,440
Of course, there is some brand value that we do get.

70
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,880
So when we do our ASO, for example, we have branded keywords.

71
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:42,240
That's part of our strategy.

72
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:43,680
And then unbranded keywords.

73
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:52,800
But App Store is different from anything else in the sense that a startup can start in Bangalore and start taking the spot on some keyword and start showing up above Word.

74
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:56,000
We have to be at our best game.

75
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:00,720
And we try to retain at least the top three spots in most of the categories.

76
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:11,280
And to your other question, being a massive brand, so think of this as like startups are kind of like department stores and we are like the cost co.

77
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:12,800
So we have all the scenarios.

78
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:22,480
We have, I mean, if you look at our Microsoft 365 app specifically, like it has Word, Excel, PowerPoint notes, PDF signing and this scanner.

79
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:25,760
And so there are so many scenarios that are there.

80
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:29,840
So from a value perspective, the value density is very high for our app.

81
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:40,560
So for like 9,99 for the family and 6,99 for personal, just the value that we can pack into one app and those prices are obviously US prices.

82
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:54,960
Some countries may be different, but like just the value that we can pack into one app, it can be up to 50 to 60 percent cheaper if you go and buy all those scenarios individually versus you buy like a M 365 subscription.

83
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:01,920
I know I'm giving a very department store type of methodology, but like you just see how much value we can pack into one app, it just blows.

84
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,840
If you try to buy, say, a scanning subscription, et cetera, et cetera, it will cost you a lot more than buying one from Microsoft.

85
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:18,000
And the other thing that also helps us is that a lot of productivity scenarios are very onetime use.

86
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:23,520
So, for example, in one month, you will have one scanning use case.

87
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:26,480
In one month, you will have one, say, two document use case.

88
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:46,480
But when you combine everything together in one app, then your retention is maintained because you may have a scanning use case today, but you may have a document use case, then you may have So overall on a suite level, we see good retention because on a suite level, there is that retention which is maintained because of different use cases that people have a use for.

89
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:53,760
So with the App Store being so competitive, and I think this applies to any app these days, it's so commoditized.

90
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,880
There's so many apps in every category.

91
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:01,520
Like I have a weather app side project and there's like a hundred weather apps.

92
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,680
And so I'm constantly thinking, like, how do I differenti kind of stay ahead?

93
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:18,320
As Microsoft, you've got way more resources to be able to think about those kinds of things, but how do you approach that differentiation and adding enough value and kind of staying ahead of the curve?

94
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:33,040
There's no one good answer to something like this, but in general, if I have to talk about from a framework perspective, you're trying to maintain a leadership position, and this applies to, like, broader than App Store, then you have to think about neutralization.

95
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:36,800
So this is a framework that one of our VPs used to talk about a lot.

96
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:41,120
Like, so you have to keep neutralizing any competitor move, for example.

97
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:42,800
Then you have to keep differentiating.

98
00:09:43,600 --> 00:10:34,320
Then you have to keep incubating and you have to keep maintaining on so for example Apple release is dark mode, Apple release Vision Pro so we always maintain a leadership position in the sense that our apps are already on Vision Pro day one we were day one dark mode we were day one on most of the new innovations that come out like we are day one there so as soon as something gets launched in say wwdc we are thinking okay how does it apply to and then depending on the stage of your app like if you are a mature app versus a startup you can like do different percentages like now say we do 30 percent incubation because we are a more mature app in the past we were more botherothered by neutralization and differentiation, but now we can just do a lot more incubation because we have a base and now we are focusing on okay, how can we bring AI?

99
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:42,320
How can we bring some of the more advanced features that can pretty much help people do complete document creation on their mobile?

100
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:48,160
If you think about what AI can do these days, like you don't need ten tabs.

101
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:53,840
You can just pull all the information like one place and create any artifact like a document video, et cetera.

102
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:55,040
So we are very focused on that.

103
00:10:55,280 --> 00:11:01,040
I want to talk more about AI, but before we move on, I want to re-clate that framework because I think a lot of apps can use this.

104
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:47,200
thinking about neutralizing so when you see another app come out with a very differentiated feature you might not like copy that I mean especially you know Microsoft but like as an app developer finding a way to neutralize that in that like find your own way to differentiate or have some form of functionality that handles aspects of those use cases and then differentiate make sure that you create new things that are differentiated maintain that leadership position and then incubate like constantly be like talking to users like doing user research and trying new things and it's cool to hear that like again the biggest company in the world thinks a lot like a startup and thinks through these kind of frameworks about neutralize, differentiate, maintain anything.

105
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:53,120
If you have an app on the app store, it's not like a bond you bought and it'll keep paying dividends, you know?

106
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:54,880
You have to keep working on it.

107
00:11:55,520 --> 00:12:02,480
You have to keep at it and you have to keep thinking about the next thing and the next thing and the next thing because it's a pretty competitive marketplace.

108
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:19,760
The other thing that I would also say is that when you are thinking about these four things, I think it's very important to think about them from the lens of jobs to be done rather than, like, say, this competition brought something and then we copy.

109
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:24,800
We never copy, we genuinely first see, okay, is this job even important for the customer?

110
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:28,960
So I think that's super important to have a very clear, even the largest app.

111
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:36,240
So like even if you take an app such as Word, there's a very defined set of jobs that Word needs to do for a user.

112
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:41,600
Like people should be able to create a resume, they should be able to create a note, they should be able to write a book.

113
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:45,360
Like I can list out 10 things that Word should be able to do or Excel.

114
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:49,200
Like you know, you should be able to create a spreadsheet, a budget or invoice.

115
00:12:49,520 --> 00:13:00,720
So if the app is able to do all of those things on any form factor, be it mobile, iPad or desktop, it's super important that we focus on that aspect that what are we trying to do?

116
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:06,640
And then there will always be more So we are a more horizontal, like from a product standpoint, we have a more horizontal solution.

117
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:09,440
There will always be more vertical solutions.

118
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:16,800
Like, for example, someone would have a very, very tight invoicing app which will have many, many things like bank connections, et cetera.

119
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:24,080
I mean, Excel will not go that deep into something like this, but then, of course, there could be add-ins that can support something like that.

120
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:28,560
So there is that horizontal versus vertical focus that you have to think about.

121
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:33,840
You can't go too focused on one vertical if you're trying to build a more horizontal presence.

122
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:34,080
So.

123
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:34,800
Okay.

124
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:36,560
So now I do want to get to AI.

125
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:36,880
Okay.

126
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:39,680
I think, you know, so many people, I mean, it's such a buzzword.

127
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:41,920
There's so many AI apps doing really well.

128
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:46,560
And so many people thinking, like, what should our AI strategy be as an app?

129
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:59,520
And I think too many people are throwing it in as a gimmick and not like deeply thinking through, like, what does this new paradigm of AI mean for our app?

130
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:13,760
And how can we introduce it into the app in a way that actually increases the value and like people actually use it instead of just being like some kind of gimmick you throw in so that your title can say whatever, whatever AI.

131
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:17,440
And then Microsoft, it's like you get working on Copilot.

132
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:34,960
pilot and then being at Microsoft you've got like a front row seat but not only that but you work with a lot of teams across the company user research and other folks to really understand how to take the best of AI and implement them in a thoughtful way that actually brings value.

133
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:36,560
So what's that approach?

134
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:42,640
Like how do you take this new hot technology and actually make something meaningful from it?

135
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:46,480
Yeah, so I mean, first of all, I'm myself kind of new to AI.

136
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:53,600
And secondly, whatever I will say will be irrelevant in one month because it's going so fast right now and things are changing so fast.

137
00:14:54,480 --> 00:15:38,080
But I mean, let's just hone down particularly into say generative AI like AI is a very broad term like there's machine learning there's that but like generative AI has a few things at least and that's my opinion like it has a few things that are interesting I think one of them is summarization it can really summarize so like meetings can get summarized for example or any document can get summarized it can do an outline based creation so like you can give an outline and some of these things are already sort of available in Microsoft 365 now so outline to document is something and it's also a change in habit that has to happen so like I think the features and the capabilities are there like people also need to now start to learn how to use them.

138
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,960
There's a big emerging prompt engineering field out there.

139
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:46,800
One more thing which I find very interesting is RAG, the Retrieval Augmented Generation.

140
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:59,600
So at least in the context of, say, a document or a note type of scenario, you can potentially pull a bunch of information on a smaller device now from different parts of the web.

141
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:05,760
So like in the past, if you were trying to say author something, you're going to Wikipedia, you're going to like ten websites.

142
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,600
Now you can ask Copilot to bring some of that information to you and also reference it and so on.

143
00:16:11,760 --> 00:16:15,920
So I think retrieval augmented generation is pretty powerful and people are using it.

144
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:21,760
I've seen many startups that are using it very successfully and that could be a pretty big thing.

145
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:26,880
And then there are some of the other scenarios like translation, which has been around for a while now.

146
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:36,800
So I think it's very, very important that in the context of whatever your app you're building, how do these new capabilities help?

147
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:43,040
Because there's a problem map, like how I talked about jobs to be done, and then there's a solution map.

148
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:46,240
So in the past, we used to have create, read, update, delete.

149
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:53,040
Like if you think about it, most of the apps were kind of like CRUD apps, and then databases came, Kafka, like big data came.

150
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,720
And so capabilities increased, you could process a lot of data.

151
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:57,840
And now it's AI.

152
00:16:58,080 --> 00:17:04,960
So I think it's an increase in solution map, but ultimately it's on the developers on how they make it useful for the jobs to be done.

153
00:17:05,120 --> 00:17:06,960
Like you can throw in a bunch of AI.

154
00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:14,000
Like most apps just go and integrate with OpenAI's API, but like there has to be some meaning.

155
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:18,880
It's almost like saying that, oh, I have an app and there's this new database.

156
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:26,960
Like ten years back, twenty years back, there were apps that would just do some kind of read operation or write operation on database because databases were cool at the time.

157
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:27,360
Right.

158
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:37,120
And so similarly, AI is cool at this time, but ultimately the companies that win will have to find a very meaningful use case that uses these.

159
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:45,920
There will be some companies that will not find a use case because for some companies summarization or RAG is not relevant and that's okay.

160
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:47,520
It's not the end of the world.

161
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:49,360
I think there's a lot of hubris, right?

162
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:53,840
Like if I don't do anything with AI, my investor will not fund me, my career will go nowhere.

163
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,600
So I think that's a very scared approach.

164
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:58,400
The problem map and solution have to align.

165
00:17:59,120 --> 00:18:03,600
One of the things we talked about a few weeks ago was, I just love the way you framed it.

166
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:11,280
And this is the way I think a lot of people could think about how to bring AI into an app is that whatever somebody might want.

167
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:16,400
to go to their computer to do, how can AI help do that in an app?

168
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:19,280
Like how can we bring more desktop class experience?

169
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:22,800
And a great example I've seen in the market is Photoroom.

170
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:24,800
They do background removal.

171
00:18:24,960 --> 00:18:27,680
It's like historically that was like you got to go into Photoshop.

172
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:34,480
You're not going to do that on your phone, you're going to do that on a desktop with a big monitor and you're going to have the tool and you're going to like do the outline.

173
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:35,760
It was like a ton of work.

174
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:40,240
And Photoroom came along and just makes it like seconds inside an app.

175
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:50,240
And so I like that framework of thinking of like how do we bring this desktop class productivity into mobile on the small screen by using these new tools and these new capabilities.

176
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:55,360
And again, like you said, it's not just about chat GPT style large language models.

177
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:01,680
It's also about using other algorithms and other technologies that are springing up around the chat GPT.

178
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:12,400
So what are some other examples at Microsoft of bringing those desktop class experiences into mobile that has shown to be really useful to customers?

179
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:21,120
I mean, so far what I talked about, like summarization, like meeting summarization, RA, like retrieval augmented generation, those are interesting scenarios.

180
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:25,200
And I mean, in consumer, there are a lot of things that people are trying.

181
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:27,840
And some with more success, some with less success.

182
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:33,760
Some funny example is like you can now create a song for your loved one and it will be in any language.

183
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:36,960
You can go to Bing and just tell it to create a song for you.

184
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:39,360
And it's a little gimmicky, but it's a real thing.

185
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:46,080
I'm sure some developers will probably find a use case for something like that algorithm because that algorithm can do things.

186
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:52,000
I don't think that right now any real songs are getting created by AI, but that day will happen.

187
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:54,560
And so I think that's a more consumery thing.

188
00:19:54,880 --> 00:20:02,160
On the more business side, I think, yeah, meeting transcriptions, recapping meetings, those are some very interesting scenarios.

189
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:08,480
Like you enter a meeting a little bit late, you can get a recap of what has happened, your meeting notes are sent to you.

190
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:53,440
task items are sent to you so I think those are like good examples of things that are actually useful because now you don't need that and I think one more which is very interesting which I tried is writing status updates so essentially I do my work in word files, Excel files, et cetera and then copilot can actually pull a lot of that information and write a status on a given topic so if I can prompt it properly it will write a whole status that I can then send out to the team or at least write 80 percent of it which is pretty good like it's a big time saver I think it just becomes super useful when it gets connected with all the apps that you are working on and then it can pull data from your context because that's live data that it's pulling and then it can really help you write content.

191
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:55,680
A lot of people are writing books these days with AI.

192
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:58,800
I think you may have seen like author plus AI.

193
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:07,600
So, those specific examples are not going to apply to a lot of apps, but I think the thinking behind it is how you have to think about it.

194
00:21:07,760 --> 00:21:21,040
And a couple of other examples you had shared with me were slide generation in PowerPoint, like being able on your phone to create a more sophisticated presentation quickly using AI and then getting insights from Excel.

195
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,440
Like if you're on a desktop computer, what are you going to do?

196
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:32,320
You're going to sit there with your keyboard and mouse and create complex equations and you're going to reference web to figure out, you know, how do I do this exact equation in Excel?

197
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:39,040
And now in Excel, there's capabilities to use AI to get some insights from that data.

198
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:42,160
And so the way to think about it is that, like, jobs to be done.

199
00:21:42,320 --> 00:21:46,320
It's like, what are the things that people would do on desktop that they can do in the app?

200
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:48,080
And I thought those were some really good examples.

201
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:52,880
The other thing I wanted to talk about is your approach to growth and the way you think about it.

202
00:21:53,040 --> 00:22:00,480
And again, I was like surprised when we started talking that your team just in the past few months implemented an MMP for the first time.

203
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:15,920
And so you think, you know, Microsoft's got everything together and like the biggest company out there, they've got all the resourources, they're super sophisticated, but like you're on the learning curve like a lot of the people here in the audience and who are going to be listening on the podcast, of like, okay, we need to find new channels to explore.

204
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:24,640
So how do you think about growth and starting to really push, as you've all seen more and more success in selling these solutions to consumers on the App Store?

205
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:25,840
How do you approach that growth?

206
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:43,680
I think one of the things which is, and I'll specifically keep it to sort of mobile and Mac and app stores for this talk, but I mean, we see the app stores as the channel which can bring us a lot of organic traffic and it can enable frictionless in app purchase, for example, right?

207
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:49,280
And to grow that, we are doing pretty much what all of the other people are doing.

208
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:52,560
And you're right, like we're not as advanced as one would think.

209
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:55,200
Like, so we have a pretty good ASO story in general.

210
00:22:55,680 --> 00:23:00,880
We have user research that kind of informs us what kind of scenarios are important.

211
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:04,400
We also get a lot like Gen Z trend and those kinds of things.

212
00:23:04,640 --> 00:23:08,960
And then we're able to take that and put it in our keyword optimization.

213
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:12,160
And then we also have a jobs to be done approach.

214
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:18,080
Like we know, okay, we think that, okay, these are the ten things that we really want our apps to rank for.

215
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:25,040
And because it's a very broad app, we have over time also become good at, okay, what are the things that people are willing to pay for.

216
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:32,400
So I think it's important to know that and just going a little bit deeper into keywords, like what kind of customers do you want to acquire?

217
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:38,880
The ones that will actually find value enough that they will want to pay for the premium subscription.

218
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:43,520
So we are getting better there and then we are integrating with an MMP.

219
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:51,760
I mean, I think one of the things that happened in the 2015 to 2018 era, we got MMP, we got an engagement platform, et cetera.

220
00:23:52,720 --> 00:23:59,840
And we kind of misimplemented some of that because one of the things, I mean, it wasn't, it was a combination of a lot of things.

221
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:02,160
So what happened in that time, like privacy.

222
00:24:02,800 --> 00:24:07,520
laws changed a lot, like GDPR came along, and we are under more scrutiny than any other app.

223
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:09,120
And we have enterprise customers.

224
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:10,320
So we sell one app.

225
00:24:10,880 --> 00:24:13,760
It's one app for all customers, enterprise and consumers.

226
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:28,240
So I think the implementations, they were not done in such a way that it could handle GDPR and handle, and also like a lot of the software companies, most of them in this conference, like they were also pretty young back then and the solutions weren't very advanced.

227
00:24:28,720 --> 00:24:36,320
So at that time we had an MMP, we had an engagement platform and one of the things that we kept facing was actually two things we kept facing.

228
00:24:36,480 --> 00:24:40,000
One was privacy issues because privacy laws were changing really, really fast.

229
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:53,760
And the second thing that we also faced at that time was just like value that we were getting from this software because we were paying these big bills because we had a very big monthly active user base even at that time.

230
00:24:54,240 --> 00:25:06,640
So we were paying these big bills but we were not really using it because on one side we were just figuring out the products because at that time like the products were not at a place where you could start doing optimization.

231
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:11,600
So optimization comes much after your product market fit is there.

232
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:20,160
So we obviously had product market fit in a big way on desktop but it took some years to kind of get that same stickiness on mobile.

233
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:51,440
where we saw okay our mobile apps our iPad apps are now like at a point where we can now start doing optimization so I think now the pendulum is turning again now we are okay we are at a point where the apps have good product market fit and now we are optimizing so MMP is there ASO strategy is there we try to use all of Apple's promotional offers promo codes and contingent pricing is a new thing we're looking into that so we we try to be as up to date with whatever we can do with these apps.

234
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:57,840
One of the problems that we always run into is just justifying the business case for some of these things.

235
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:07,760
So when you have an app that has double or triple digit million Mao, then most of the time the magnitude of opportunity has to be very high.

236
00:26:08,320 --> 00:26:13,760
So it's like the team size doesn't grow the same way how your Mao is growing, right?

237
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
Like because also team size cannot grow.

238
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:21,680
You cannot like because like to add one engineer or say to add ten engineers, you also need a manager.

239
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:24,160
Your team size doesn't exactly grow and all of you know that.

240
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:26,560
So our thinking has to keep growing.

241
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:41,120
As our Mao is growing, we have to think bigger and bigger and our prioritization has to be more and more strict because like if you have double or triple digit million Mao, you can't be spendinging time on an optimization that will impact one million users.

242
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:45,040
You have to be spending time on something that will impact, say, fifty or hundred, whatever.

243
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:46,080
It's a big number.

244
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:50,800
So our framework, our VP is very, very strict about this, like estimated impact.

245
00:26:51,360 --> 00:26:58,560
Any kind of vendor we have to bring in or anything that we have to do, any kind of optimization, he's like, okay, give me the estimated impact.

246
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:02,800
And then we have this huge list of ideas that come from all sorts of places.

247
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:05,120
And then we're like, okay, let's triage.

248
00:27:05,280 --> 00:27:10,640
And a lot of times you'll be surprised that the biggest ideas are the simplest ones.

249
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:16,640
Like, okay, let's do something on purchase for the first time or let's do something on the first time experience.

250
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:30,560
Because if you have an app that big, then sometimes the simplest things like performance, reliability, file open speed, size of the app, those things matter, start to matter a lot.

251
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:34,400
Are we able to 100% transaction completion?

252
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:41,120
Because with that kind of numbers, at one point we were seeing 5, 6 percent transaction failure.

253
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:42,640
So we first have to fix that.

254
00:27:43,120 --> 00:27:45,520
So I think that's where it becomes different from startups.

255
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:47,760
It's not all that different though.

256
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:55,680
And that's what I was thinking the whole time you were talking is that I think too many small apps don't have that prioritization framework in place.

257
00:27:56,240 --> 00:27:57,360
I hear apps all the time.

258
00:27:57,520 --> 00:28:02,960
They're at like $100,000 a month in revenue, you know, like orders of magnitude smaller than what you're working on.

259
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:07,600
But then they go try and like, oh, we gotta have win back campaigns.

260
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:16,320
It's like, well, if you're only at $100,000 a month and you only have thousands or tens of thousands of users, like that's not low hanging fruit.

261
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:25,680
And so from the smallest apps in the app store to the biggest apps in the app store, you really should be thinking about these prioritization frameworks.

262
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:37,920
And you should, every feature that you're working on, every tool that you're going to implement, every strategy that you're going to put in place, you should be thinking and have a hypothesis, like what's the real impact going to be for our business?

263
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:41,840
And again, if you're like, oh yeah, we gotta do winbacks, like that's a huge thing.

264
00:28:42,240 --> 00:28:59,520
Well, okay, if a thousand people are going to turn from your app over the next six months and you can hope to win back five to ten percent, like how meaningful of an impact to your business is that compared to like so many other opportunities that you can have much higher impact to your business?

265
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:01,840
I think that's actually a great way to think about it.

266
